  "query_preview": "..(Sunday through Friday)\nIssued at 543 PM EDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nWhen the long term forecast period opens on Sunday morning, there \nmay be ongoing showers and thunderstorms across Northeastern \nKentucky. That activity is associated with a subtle shortwave \ndisturbance propagating around the base of the broad mid/upper level \ntroughing centered over the Great Lakes region. That troughing is \nprogged to flatten out and lift north of the forecast area as the \nday progresses, with midlevel height rises and quasi-zonal flow \nobserved across the commonwealth in its wake. Barring any \nparticularly stubborn cloud debris from the AM convection, such a \nsetup favors efficient diurnal warming. Sunday's highs have trended \na few degrees upwards compared to previous forecast packages, \nespecially in southern and western locales. Expect highs in the \nmid/upper 80s, but a few readings near 90 cannot be ruled out. Such \ntemperatures should be sufficient enough for another round of \nconvection to blossom in the second half of the forecast area later \nthat afternoon/evening. Sunday's PoPs have accordingly risen towards \nscattered coverage values (25-45%) in the southern half of our CWA, \nbut any thunderstorms would likely be of the garden variety (sub-\nsevere). They will still pose a risk for locally gusty winds, \nlightning, and torrential downpours, but widespread impactful \nweather is not anticipated. \n\nWith the return of quasi-zonal flow aloft, a weak surface pressure \ngradient, and the increased potential for antecedently wet grounds, \nSunday night is shaping up to be a candidate for nocturnal radiation \nfog. Confidence in fog formation is greatest in the typically-\nimpacted river valleys, but patchier fog may develop elsewhere in \nsuch a setup. Expect fog to begin to mix out during the Monday \nmorning commute, setting up a generally pleasant, but hot, late \nsummer day in Eastern Kentucky. Most of the area will experience a \nreprieve from the recent excessive rainfall risks on Monday, with \nonly isolated showers and storms expected along the terrain in SE \nKY. Continued midlevel height rises and strengthening southwesterly \nlow level flow will yield highs in the upper 80s/lower 90s. \nDewpoints in the 70s combine with these temperatures to yield \nwidespread heat indices in the mid to upper 90s. A few readings near \n100 degrees cannot be ruled out in urbanized areas and in the \ndeepest valley locales.  \n\nThe attention then turns towards a series of ridge-riding shortwave \nimpulses approaching the forecast area between Tuesday and Friday. \nThe presence of these disturbances in the coarse long term guidance \ncauses scattered to numerous coverage-level PoPs to reenter the \nforecast by midweek, but it remains difficult to pinpoint the exact \ntemporal oscillations within those PoPs. Pattern recognition \ncontinues to suggest that next week's convection could take the form \nof MCSes moving through the Ohio River Valley. The available medium-\nrange machine learning models and analog-based convective guidance \nstill suggest that a risk for damaging wind gusts could materialize \nin the late Tuesday to Wednesday time frame. This will likely be \naccompanied by a threat for heavy rainfall, and WPC has already \ndelineated a Slight (Level 2/4) Excessive Rainfall Outlook for \nTuesday. Depending on the evolution of each successive round of \nconvection, a risk for flash flooding could materialize across the \ncommonwealth. Confidence in forecast details like rainfall totals \nand favored corridors for training storms/backbuilding is low at the \ncurrent temporal range, so interests are encouraged to monitor for \nupdates in the coming days. On the plus side, increased sky cover \nshould help quell the heat in the latter stages of the work week. \nExpect temperatures to modulate back down towards seasonably \nappropriate highs in the 80s and lows in the 60s by the end of the \nperiod on Friday.",
